<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">
    <div class="guide-toc">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#problem">Why does selection on reputation go wrong?</a>
      <a href="#data">What do club-night results tell a captain?</a>
      <a href="#pairings">How do you find the pairs that work?</a>
      <a href="#enough">How many games make a pairing trustworthy?</a>
      <a href="#example">A worked selection</a>
      <a href="#limits">When should the data not decide?</a>
      <a href="#balance">Should you stack the first team?</a>
      <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">
      <p class="lead"><strong>Pick league pairs on how they have actually played together, not on how strong each player looks alone.</strong> Rank the squad by current club-night results, then choose pairs from their record as a pair, and treat any pairing with only a handful of games together as unproven. Keep the captain's judgement for what results cannot see: availability, reliability, singles and tactics.</p>

      <p>Get it wrong and the sheet goes up on Monday, the player who has been beating your second pair all month is on it as reserve, and the club WhatsApp group goes quiet in the way that means people are messaging each other privately instead.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <h4>Key points</h4>
        <ul style="margin:10px 0 0">
          <li>Reputation lags results. Current club-night form is the better guide to who should play.</li>
          <li>Doubles is won by pairs. Your two strongest players are not automatically your strongest pair.</li>
          <li>A pair's record together is the evidence; a pair with only a few games together is unknown, not weak.</li>
          <li>Results cannot see availability, reliability, singles or match-day tactics. Those stay with the captain.</li>
          <li>Selection you can explain from results is easier to defend than selection you cannot.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem">For captains picking doubles sides for a local or district league, at UK club level. It assumes your club nights record scores and who partnered whom.</p>

      <h2 id="problem">Why does selection on reputation go wrong?</h2>
      <p>Selection on reputation goes wrong because reputations change more slowly than players do. The player who was strongest two seasons ago keeps their place while an improver is overlooked, and a pairing put together once out of convenience is never questioned. Because none of it is written down, every disagreement becomes one opinion against another, and nobody can win that argument.</p>

      <h2 id="data">What do club-night results tell a captain?</h2>
      <p>Club-night results tell a captain three things: who is in form now, how each player's standard compares with the rest of the squad, and which pairs win more or less than their individual standards predict. A few weeks of ordinary club nights is a large sample of competitive doubles, far larger than the handful of league matches anyone plays in the same period.</p>
      <p>You do not need a spreadsheet. You need the scores recorded with the four names on each game, which any session system or a disciplined paper sheet can do. A <a href="/guides/badminton-club-rating-systems">club rating</a> then turns those results into a single number per player that can be compared.</p>

      <h2 id="pairings">How do you find the pairs that work?</h2>
      <p>You find the pairs that work by comparing each pair's results together with what their individual standards predict. Two strong players who both like to play at the front can lose to a weaker pair whose styles fit, and only their results as a pair will show it.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Over-performing pair:</strong> wins more often than their two ratings suggest. Keep them together.</li>
        <li><strong>Under-performing pair:</strong> two good players who lose more than they should together. Try splitting them before dropping either.</li>
        <li><strong>A player who lifts partners:</strong> several different partners do better with them than without. Worth a place even when their own rating is middling.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="enough">How many games make a pairing trustworthy?</h2>
      <p>A pairing needs something like ten games together before its record means much. Three wins from three is a good evening, not evidence; across ten or more games the luck of the draw starts to even out. Below that, judge the pair on their individual standards and say that is what you are doing.</p>
      <p>The same applies to a player who has recently joined. Their rating will move quickly for the first dozen or so games, so treat it as provisional until it settles.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" id="example">
        <h4>A worked selection</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px"><em>An illustrative squad, not real players.</em> The first team plays three men's pairs; eight players are available. Club-night ratings: Amir 1720, Ben 1700, Chris 1660, Dev 1650, Ed 1630, Femi 1610, Gus 1600, Hal 1590.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">On ratings alone the team is Amir to Femi, paired one and two, three and four, five and six. The club-night record adds three facts:</p>
        <ul style="margin:8px 0 0">
          <li>Amir and Ben have 12 games together and win less often than their ratings predict.</li>
          <li>Ed and Gus have 15 games together and have beaten their prediction all season.</li>
          <li>Femi has partnered Hal only three times, so there is no pair record to go on.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Pick Amir with Dev, Ben with Chris, and Ed with Gus. Femi goes to the second team despite a higher rating than Gus, and the captain can say why in one sentence: Ed and Gus's fifteen games together.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="limits">When should the data not decide?</h2>
      <p>The data should not decide on anything club nights do not measure. Results cannot tell you who will turn up for an away match on a wet Thursday, who plays well under league pressure, or who is carrying an injury. They say almost nothing about singles if your club nights are doubles, and nothing about a mixed pair who have never played together.</p>
      <p>For some clubs the honest answer is that no data is needed. If eight people are available for six places and everyone agrees who the six are, pick them and spend the evening on something else.</p>

      <h2 id="balance">Should you stack the first team?</h2>
      <p>You should not stack the first team by default, because each team plays in its own division and a weak lower team loses its matches whatever the first team does. Decide what each team needs to stay competitive in its division, then share players out. A team that is promoted on borrowed strength usually comes straight back down. Running the league side of this, divisions included, is covered in <a href="/guides/how-to-run-a-local-badminton-league">how to run a local badminton league</a>.</p>

